# Content pipeline files (one JSON file per world entity, git-as-source-of-truth).
# Generated pure-LF with canonical serialization by scripts/content/export.mjs;
# byte-identity across machines is what makes exports and merge conflicts sane,
# so never let autocrlf touch them. audio_samples carry one-line base64 blobs, so
# they also get -diff — rendering a multi-MB single-line change is useless noise.
#
# merge=contentjson routes these through scripts/content/merge-json.mjs, which
# merges by KEY instead of by line. These files are canonically serialized, so
# line adjacency is an artifact of Object.keys().sort() and means nothing — two
# authors editing unrelated fields land on neighbouring lines and git's text
# merge calls it a conflict. Merging main into studio-manager produced 93 such
# conflicts; 82 of them were edits that compose perfectly. The driver has to be
# registered per clone (git config supplies the command, this file only names
# it) — see the header of merge-json.mjs for the two lines. A clone that hasn't
# registered it falls back to the text merge, which is noisier but never wrong.
content/**/*.json text eol=lf merge=contentjson
content/audio_samples/*.json text eol=lf -diff merge=contentjson

# Batch files MUST be CRLF. cmd.exe resolves `call :label` by BYTE OFFSET, so an
# LF-only .bat jumps to the wrong line mid-run — blocks get skipped and re-executed
# (this bit scripts/oneshots.bat during authoring). Never let autocrlf leave these LF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
